# Server-sent events (the WHATWG HTML Living Standard, §9.2): a stream of
# text events over one long HTTP response, `text/event-stream`, for both
# ends of std/http.
#
# **The server.** Inside `Server.serve`'s block, `SSE.stream { |events|
# ... }` answers the request: a 200 with `Content-Type:
# text/event-stream` and `Cache-Control: no-cache` (a caller's own
# `Cache-Control` replaces it; its own `Content-Type` is refused), whose
# body the block writes as it goes through `Response.stream`.
# `events.send(data, event: nil, id: nil, retry: nil)` writes one event -
# its `event:`, `id:` and `retry:` lines, then its data a `data:` line per
# line, then the blank line that dispatches it - as one chunk, sent at
# once; `events.comment(text)` writes `:` lines, which a client reads
# past, and which are what keeps a quiet stream's proxies from dropping
# it (§9.2.7). A line break in data is three things on the way in (CRLF,
# CR, LF) and arrives as LF, since the format has no way to say which it
# was. What would break the stream is refused before it is written: a
# line break in an event type, a line break or a NUL in an id (a client
# ignores an id with a NUL, §9.2.6), a negative retry, and text that is
# not UTF-8, which is the only encoding the format has (§9.2.5). A client
# that went is not a panic: the write that finds it answers the
# `SocketError`, every write after it answers the same, and `gone?` says
# so, so the block's loop ends on a value. A client is only found gone
# when something is written to it; a comment is the cheapest thing that
# can be.
#
# **The client.** `SSE.open(url) { |event| ... }` is `EventSource`
# without a browser: a GET through `HTTP.open` with `Accept:
# text/event-stream` and `Cache-Control: no-cache` (the request's
# `no-store` cache mode, as Fetch writes it), and `Last-Event-ID` when
# the last event ID is not empty. The block gets each `Event` - its type
# (`message` when the stream named none), its data, and the last event
# ID - and answers true to go on or false to stop, which closes the
# connection and answers nil. An answer that is not a 200, or whose
# `Content-Type` is not `text/event-stream` (parameters allowed), fails
# the connection as the standard has it: no reconnection, and an
# `SSEError` naming what came. A connection that drops or ends is
# reconnected after the reconnection time - *retry* milliseconds, 3000
# unless the caller says, until the stream's own `retry:` field sets it -
# with the last event ID in `Last-Event-ID`, as a browser does. A browser
# goes on for ever; here, once *reconnects* + 1 connections in a row (5
# by default, so six) - the first or a reconnection - have brought no
# event, `open` answers the last one's failure (`SocketError`,
# `HTTPError`, or an `SSEError` for a stream that ended cleanly); a
# connection that brings one starts the count again. There is no backoff
# past the reconnection time, which §9.2.3 leaves to the user agent.
#
# Two decisions where the standard's text and a browser differ or are
# silent. The last event ID buffer of a new connection's stream starts
# from the last event ID, not empty: §9.2.6 initializes each stream's
# buffers to the empty string, which would make the first event of a
# resumed stream that carries no `id:` reset the ID and lose the place,
# and Chromium's `EventSource` hands the ID to the new parser ("The new
# parser takes over the event ID", `event_source.cc`). And an ID that
# holds a control character other than a tab is kept and handed to the
# block but not sent: std/http refuses such a byte in any header value,
# and a server that sent one should not make its client panic.
#
# **The parser.** `EventParser` is §9.2.6's interpretation, fed bytes as
# they arrive in pieces of any size: UTF-8 decoded with ill-formed bytes
# as U+FFFD (std/unicode's `clean`, the maximal-subpart rule the Encoding
# Standard's decoder follows), one leading BOM dropped, lines ended by
# CRLF, LF or CR - a CR at the end of one piece and an LF at the start of
# the next being one CRLF -, a line that starts with `:` read past, a
# line without a colon a field with an empty value, one space after the
# colon dropped, field names compared exactly. `data` lines are joined
# with LF, the last LF removed; an `id` with a NUL is ignored; a `retry`
# of ASCII digits alone sets the reconnection time (saturating at
# 2147483647 ms, about 24 days, past what `sleep` takes); a blank line
# sets the last event ID and dispatches the event when its data is not
# empty, and what a stream ends with before its blank line is discarded.
# `bench/std_sse_exercise.sh` holds it to the standard's own examples and
# to a Python transcription of the algorithm over a generated corpus fed
# in pieces of one to seven bytes, and runs the client against a Python
# server that drops the connection mid-stream, and the server under
# Python's and curl's reading.
#
# Not here: a limit on a line or an event's size (a browser has none
# either; a stream is as long as the server makes it), `withCredentials`
# and CORS, which are a browser's, and a client that reconnects with
# backoff.

module std/sse

import std/http::{HTTP, Response, BodyWriter, HTTPError}
import std/socket::{SocketError}
import std/mime::{MIME}

# What the other end did that fails an event stream (§9.2.3), as a value:
# an answer that is not a 200, one that is not `text/event-stream`, or a
# stream that kept ending with no event until the client gave up.
# `status` is the answer's. `message` says it is SSE's.
pub struct SSEError
  getter reason : String
  getter status : Int32

  def initialize(@reason : String, @status : Int32 = 200)
  end

  def to_s : String
    "SSE: " + @reason
  end
end

impl Error for SSEError
  def message : String
    "SSE: " + @reason
  end
end

# One dispatched event: its type (`message` unless the stream named one
# with `event:`), its data, and the last event ID as it stood when it was
# dispatched, which an event without an `id:` field inherits.
pub struct Event
  getter event : String
  getter data : String
  getter id : String

  def initialize(@event : String, @data : String, @id : String)
  end
end

# §9.2.6's interpretation of an event stream, fed its bytes as they come:
# `feed(bytes) { |event| ... }` yields each event a blank line
# dispatches, and keeps what a piece ended inside - a line, an event, the
# CR of a CRLF - for the next. `finish` discards it, as the end of a
# stream does. `last_event_id` is the source's last event ID string,
# set at each blank line; `retry` the reconnection time the stream set,
# nil until it sets one; `dispatched` the events yielded so far.
pub class EventParser
  MAX_RETRY = 2147483647
  getter last_event_id : String
  getter retry : Int32?
  getter dispatched : Int64
  @id_buffer : String
  @type : String
  @data : String
  @line : String
  @after_cr : Bool
  @first : Bool

  def initialize(@last_event_id : String = "")
    @id_buffer = @last_event_id
    @type = ""
    @data = ""
    @line = ""
    @after_cr = false
    @first = true
    @retry = nil
    @dispatched = 0_i64
  end

  def feed(text : String, & : Event -> Nil) : Nil
    tape do
      parser = TapeEventParser.new(@last_event_id.as_tape)
      parser.restore(@id_buffer.as_tape, @type.as_tape,
        @data.as_tape, @line.as_tape, @after_cr, @first,
        @retry, @dispatched)
      parser.feed(text.as_tape) do |event|
        copy_state(parser)
        yield event.to_heap
      end
      copy_state(parser)
    end
  end

  private def copy_state(parser : TapeEventParser) : Nil
    @last_event_id = parser.last_event_id.to_heap
    @id_buffer = parser.id_buffer.to_heap
    @type = parser.type.to_heap
    @data = parser.data_buffer.to_heap
    @line = parser.line_buffer.to_heap
    @after_cr = parser.after_cr
    @first = parser.first
    @retry = parser.retry
    @dispatched = parser.dispatched
  end

  def finish : Nil
    @line = ""
    @data = ""
    @type = ""
    @after_cr = false
  end
end

@[OnTape]
pub struct TapeEvent
  getter event : Tape::String
  getter data : Tape::String
  getter id : Tape::String

  def initialize(@event : Tape::String, @data : Tape::String,
                 @id : Tape::String)
  end

  def to_heap : Event
    Event.new(@event.to_heap, @data.to_heap, @id.to_heap)
  end
end

@[OnTape]
pub struct TapeEventParser
  @last_event_id : Pointer(Tape::String)

  def last_event_id : Tape::String
    @last_event_id.value
  end

  @retry : Pointer(Int32?)

  def retry : Int32?
    @retry.value
  end

  @dispatched : Pointer(Int64)

  def dispatched : Int64
    @dispatched.value
  end

  @id_buffer : Pointer(Tape::String)

  def id_buffer : Tape::String
    @id_buffer.value
  end

  @type : Pointer(Tape::String)

  def type : Tape::String
    @type.value
  end

  @data : Pointer(Tape::String::Writer)

  def data : Tape::String::Writer
    @data.value
  end

  @line : Pointer(Tape::String::Writer)

  def line : Tape::String::Writer
    @line.value
  end

  @after_cr : Pointer(Bool)

  def after_cr : Bool
    @after_cr.value
  end

  @first : Pointer(Bool)

  def first : Bool
    @first.value
  end

  # The largest reconnection time kept, in milliseconds: what `sleep`
  # takes.
  MAX_RETRY = 2147483647

  # A parser for a stream whose last event ID is *last_event_id* so far:
  # a reconnection's, which carries the one before it (see the module's
  # header for why).
  def initialize(last_event_id : Tape::String = "".as_tape)
    @last_event_id = Pointer(Tape::String).new(
      IyiRegion.tape_bytes(sizeof(Tape::String).to_u64))
    @retry = Pointer(Int32?).new(
      IyiRegion.tape_bytes(sizeof(Int32?).to_u64))
    @dispatched = Pointer(Int64).new(
      IyiRegion.tape_bytes(sizeof(Int64).to_u64))
    @id_buffer = Pointer(Tape::String).new(
      IyiRegion.tape_bytes(sizeof(Tape::String).to_u64))
    @type = Pointer(Tape::String).new(
      IyiRegion.tape_bytes(sizeof(Tape::String).to_u64))
    @data = Pointer(Tape::String::Writer).new(
      IyiRegion.tape_bytes(sizeof(Tape::String::Writer).to_u64))
    @line = Pointer(Tape::String::Writer).new(
      IyiRegion.tape_bytes(sizeof(Tape::String::Writer).to_u64))
    @after_cr = Pointer(Bool).new(
      IyiRegion.tape_bytes(sizeof(Bool).to_u64))
    @first = Pointer(Bool).new(
      IyiRegion.tape_bytes(sizeof(Bool).to_u64))
    @last_event_id.value = last_event_id
    @id_buffer.value = @last_event_id.value
    @type.value = "".as_tape
    @data.value = Tape::String::Writer.new(64)
    @line.value = Tape::String::Writer.new(64)
    @after_cr.value = false
    @first.value = true
    @retry.value = nil
    @dispatched.value = 0_i64
  end

  def data_buffer : Tape::String
    @data.value.finish
  end

  def line_buffer : Tape::String
    @line.value.finish
  end

  def restore(id : Tape::String, type : Tape::String,
              data : Tape::String, line : Tape::String,
              after_cr : Bool, first : Bool, retry : Int32?,
              dispatched : Int64) : Nil
    @id_buffer.value = id
    @type.value = type
    @data.value << data
    @line.value << line
    @after_cr.value = after_cr
    @first.value = first
    @retry.value = retry
    @dispatched.value = dispatched
  end

  def feed(text : Tape::String, & : TapeEvent -> Nil) : Nil
    p = text
    n = text.bytesize
    i = 0
    # A CR that ended the last piece was a line's end already; an LF that
    # starts this one is the rest of that CRLF, not a blank line.
    if @after_cr.value && n > 0
      @after_cr.value = false
      i = 1 if p[0] == 10_u8
    end
    start = i
    while i < n
      c = p[i]
      if c == 10_u8 || c == 13_u8
        line = text[start, i - start]
        if @line.value.length > 0
          @line.value << line
          line = @line.value.finish
          @line.value = Tape::String::Writer.new(64)
        end
        i = i + 1
        if c == 13_u8
          if i < n
            i = i + 1 if p[i] == 10_u8
          else
            @after_cr.value = true
          end
        end
        start = i
        if event = take(line)
          yield event
        end
      else
        i = i + 1
      end
    end
    @line.value << text[start, n - start] if start < n
  end

  # The end of the stream: a line or an event not yet ended is discarded,
  # not dispatched. The last event ID and the reconnection time stay.
  def finish : Nil
    @line.value = Tape::String::Writer.new(64)
    @data.value = Tape::String::Writer.new(64)
    @type.value = "".as_tape
    @after_cr.value = false
  end

  # One line, its end taken off: the event it dispatches, if it does.
  private def take(bytes : Tape::String) : TapeEvent?
    line = SSE.clean(bytes)
    if @first.value
      @first.value = false
      # The decoder's one BOM (the Encoding Standard's UTF-8 decode); a
      # second is the first character of the line.
      if line.bytesize >= 3 && line[0] == 0xEF_u8 &&
         line[1] == 0xBB_u8 && line[2] == 0xBF_u8
        line = line[3, line.bytesize - 3]
      end
    end
    return dispatch if line.bytesize == 0
    return nil if line[0] == 58_u8
    field = line
    value = "".as_tape
    if colon = line.index(':')
      field = line[0, colon]
      value = line[colon + 1, line.bytesize - colon - 1]
      if !value.empty? && value[0] == 32_u8
        value = value[1, value.bytesize - 1]
      end
    end
    if field == "event"
      @type.value = value
    elsif field == "data"
      @data.value << value << '\n'
    elsif field == "id"
      @id_buffer.value = value unless value.includes?('\0')
    elsif field == "retry"
      if ms = TapeEventParser.digits(value)
        @retry.value = ms
      end
    end
    nil
  end

  # A blank line: the last event ID is the buffer's whether or not an
  # event goes, and an event goes when it has data.
  private def dispatch : TapeEvent?
    @last_event_id.value = @id_buffer.value
    if @data.value.length == 0
      @type.value = "".as_tape
      return nil
    end
    data = @data.value.finish
    data = data[0, data.bytesize - 1] if !data.empty? && data[-1] == 10_u8
    @data.value = Tape::String::Writer.new(64)
    kind = @type.value.empty? ? "message".as_tape : @type.value
    event = TapeEvent.new(kind, data, @last_event_id.value)
    @type.value = "".as_tape
    @dispatched.value = @dispatched.value + 1_i64
    event
  end

  # A `retry` value: ASCII digits and nothing else, base ten, saturating
  # at `MAX_RETRY`; nil for anything else, the empty value included.
  def self.digits(text : Tape::String) : Int32?
    return nil if text.bytesize == 0
    p = text
    value = 0_i64
    i = 0
    while i < text.bytesize
      c = p[i]
      return nil unless c >= 48_u8 && c <= 57_u8
      value = value * 10_i64 + (c - 48_u8).to_i64
      value = MAX_RETRY.to_i64 if value > MAX_RETRY.to_i64
      i = i + 1
    end
    value.to_i32
  end
end

# What `SSE.stream`'s block writes with: an event a `send`, a comment a
# `comment`, each one chunk sent at once. The first write that fails -
# the client went - is kept, and it and every write after it answer it;
# `gone?` says whether one has.
pub class EventWriter
  @writer : BodyWriter
  @fault : Cancelled | SocketError | Nil

  def initialize(@writer : BodyWriter)
    @fault = nil
  end

  # Whether a write found the client gone.
  def gone? : Bool
    !@fault.nil?
  end

  # One event: `event:`, `id:` and `retry:` when given, *data* a `data:`
  # line per line (CRLF, CR and LF each end one, and each arrives as LF),
  # then the blank line that dispatches it. An empty *data* is an event
  # whose data is empty; an empty *id* resets the client's last event ID.
  def send(data : String, event : String? = nil, id : String? = nil, retry : Int32? = nil) : Nil | Cancelled | SocketError
    put(SSE.encode(data, event, id, retry))
  end

  def comment(text : String = "") : Nil | Cancelled | SocketError
    put(SSE.comment(text))
  end

  private def put(text : String) : Nil | Cancelled | SocketError
    if fault = @fault
      return fault
    end
    got = @writer.write(text)
    @fault = got
    got
  end
end

pub struct SSE
  def self.encode(data : String, event : String? = nil,
                  id : String? = nil, retry : Int32? = nil) : String
    result = ""
    tape do
      kind = event ? event.as_tape : nil
      mark = id ? id.as_tape : nil
      result = encode(data.as_tape, kind, mark, retry).to_heap
    end
    result
  end

  def self.encode(data : Tape::String, event : Tape::String? = nil,
                  id : Tape::String? = nil,
                  retry : Int32? = nil) : Tape::String
    Tape::String.build { |w| encode(data, w, event, id, retry) }
  end

  def self.encode(data : Tape::String, writer : Tape::String::Writer,
                  event : Tape::String? = nil, id : Tape::String? = nil,
                  retry : Int32? = nil) : Nil
    # Validate every field before changing the caller's writer.
    if kind = event
      utf8(kind, "an event type")
      if breaks?(kind)
        raise "SSE: an event type cannot hold a line break: " \
              "#{kind.to_heap.inspect}"
      end
    end
    if mark = id
      utf8(mark, "an id")
      if breaks?(mark) || mark.includes?('\0')
        raise "SSE: an id cannot hold a line break or a NUL: " \
              "#{mark.to_heap.inspect}"
      end
    end
    if ms = retry
      raise "SSE: a retry is 0 or more milliseconds, not #{ms}" if ms < 0
    end
    utf8(data, "the data")
    writer << "event: " << kind << '\n' if kind = event
    writer << "id: " << mark << '\n' if mark = id
    writer << "retry: " << ms << '\n' if ms = retry
    lines(data) do |line|
      writer << "data:"
      writer << ' ' << line unless line.empty?
      writer << '\n'
    end
    writer << '\n'
  end

  def self.comment(text : String = "") : String
    result = ""
    tape do
      result = comment(text.as_tape).to_heap
    end
    result
  end

  def self.comment(text : Tape::String) : Tape::String
    Tape::String.build { |w| comment(text, w) }
  end

  def self.comment(text : Tape::String,
                   writer : Tape::String::Writer) : Nil
    utf8(text, "a comment")
    lines(text) do |line|
      writer << ':'
      writer << ' ' << line unless line.empty?
      writer << '\n'
    end
  end

  private def self.breaks?(text : Tape::String) : Bool
    text.includes?('\n') || text.includes?('\r')
  end

  private def self.utf8(text : Tape::String, what : String) : Nil
    at = 0
    while at < text.bytesize
      width = sequence_at(text, text.bytesize, at)
      if width < 0
        raise "SSE: #{what} is not UTF-8: #{text.to_heap.inspect}"
      end
      at += width
    end
  end

  private def self.lines(text : Tape::String,
                         & : Tape::String -> Nil) : Nil
    start = 0
    at = 0
    while at < text.bytesize
      byte = text[at]
      if byte == 10_u8 || byte == 13_u8
        yield text[start, at - start]
        at += 1
        if byte == 13_u8 && at < text.bytesize && text[at] == 10_u8
          at += 1
        end
        start = at
      else
        at += 1
      end
    end
    yield text[start, text.bytesize - start]
  end

  # UTF-8 replacement uses the Encoding Standard's maximal subpart.
  def self.clean(bytes : Tape::String) : Tape::String
    at = 0
    while at < bytes.bytesize
      length = sequence_at(bytes, bytes.bytesize, at)
      break if length < 0
      at += length
    end
    return bytes if at == bytes.bytesize
    writer = Tape::String::Writer.new(bytes.bytesize)
    writer << bytes[0, at]
    while at < bytes.bytesize
      length = sequence_at(bytes, bytes.bytesize, at)
      if length < 0
        writer << "\uFFFD"
        at -= length
      else
        writer << bytes[at, length]
        at += length
      end
    end
    writer.finish
  end

  private def self.sequence_at(bytes : Tape::String,
                               size : Int32, at : Int32) : Int32
    lead = bytes[at]
    return 1 if lead < 0x80_u8
    need = 0
    low = 0x80_u8
    high = 0xBF_u8
    if lead >= 0xC2_u8 && lead <= 0xDF_u8
      need = 1
    elsif lead == 0xE0_u8
      need = 2
      low = 0xA0_u8
    elsif lead == 0xED_u8
      need = 2
      high = 0x9F_u8
    elsif lead >= 0xE1_u8 && lead <= 0xEF_u8
      need = 2
    elsif lead == 0xF0_u8
      need = 3
      low = 0x90_u8
    elsif lead == 0xF4_u8
      need = 3
      high = 0x8F_u8
    elsif lead >= 0xF1_u8 && lead <= 0xF3_u8
      need = 3
    else
      return -1
    end
    taken = 1
    while taken <= need
      return 0 - taken if at + taken >= size
      byte = bytes[at + taken]
      return 0 - taken if byte < low || byte > high
      low = 0x80_u8
      high = 0xBF_u8
      taken = taken + 1
    end
    taken
  end

  # The reconnection time a client starts with, in milliseconds.
  RETRY = 3000
  # How many reconnections a client makes after a connection that brought
  # no event, while each brings none.
  RECONNECTS = 5

  # The answer to a request for an event stream: a 200, `text/event-stream`,
  # `Cache-Control: no-cache` unless *headers* names its own, whose body
  # *block* writes through an `EventWriter` as it goes. The stream ends
  # when the block returns. A `Content-Type` in *headers* is refused.
  def self.stream(headers : Hash(String, String) = {} of String => String, &block : EventWriter -> Nil) : Response
    sent = {"Content-Type" => "text/event-stream"}
    cached = false
    headers.each do |name, value|
      raise "SSE: Content-Type is the stream's own, text/event-stream" if HTTP.same_name?(name, "Content-Type")
      cached = true if HTTP.same_name?(name, "Cache-Control")
      sent[name] = value
    end
    sent["Cache-Control"] = "no-cache" unless cached
    Response.stream(200, sent) { |writer| block.call(EventWriter.new(writer)) }
  end

  # The event stream at *url*, each event handed to *block*, which answers
  # true to go on and false to stop (nil, the connection closed). A
  # connection that drops or ends is made again after the reconnection
  # time - *retry* ms until the stream sets its own - with the last event
  # ID (*last_event_id* to start with, to resume a stream) in
  # `Last-Event-ID`. Once *reconnects* + 1 connections in a row have
  # brought no event, the last one's failure is the answer. An answer
  # that is not a 200 `text/event-stream` is an `SSEError` at once.
  # *headers* go with every request; `Accept` and `Last-Event-ID` are the
  # stream's own and are refused there.
  def self.open(url : String, headers : Hash(String, String) = {} of String => String, last_event_id : String? = nil, reconnects : Int32 = RECONNECTS, retry : Int32 = RETRY, &block : Event -> Bool) : Nil | Cancelled | SocketError | HTTPError | SSEError
    raise "SSE: reconnects is 0 or more, not #{reconnects}" if reconnects < 0
    raise "SSE: a retry is 0 or more milliseconds, not #{retry}" if retry < 0
    cached = false
    headers.each do |name, _|
      if HTTP.same_name?(name, "Accept") || HTTP.same_name?(name, "Last-Event-ID")
        raise "SSE: #{name} is the stream's own header; the last event ID is last_event_id:"
      end
      cached = true if HTTP.same_name?(name, "Cache-Control")
    end
    last = last_event_id || ""
    raise "SSE: an id cannot hold a line break or a NUL: #{last.inspect}" if HTTP.has_break?(last) || last.includes?("\0")
    delay = retry
    misses = 0
    while true
      sent = headers.dup
      sent["Accept"] = "text/event-stream"
      sent["Cache-Control"] = "no-cache" unless cached
      sent["Last-Event-ID"] = last if last.bytesize > 0 && !HTTP.control?(last)
      parser = EventParser.new(last)
      outcome = HTTP.open("GET", url, sent) { |response| SSE.follow(response, parser, block) }
      last = parser.last_event_id
      if ms = parser.retry
        delay = ms
      end
      misses = parser.dispatched > 0_i64 ? 0 : misses + 1
      case outcome
      in Bool
        return nil if outcome
        failure = SSEError.new("the stream ended with no event, #{misses} times in a row")
      in SSEError
        return outcome
      in Cancelled
        return outcome
      in SocketError
        failure = outcome
      in HTTPError
        failure = outcome
      end
      return failure if misses > reconnects
      slept = sleep(delay)
      return slept if slept.is_a?(Cancelled)
    end
  end

  # One connection's answer read: true when the block stopped, false when
  # the stream ended, or why it could not be read.
  protected def self.follow(response : Response, parser : EventParser, block : Proc(Event, Bool)) : Bool | SSEError | Cancelled | SocketError | HTTPError
    status = response.status
    return SSEError.new("the server answered #{status}, not 200", status) unless status == 200
    type = response.header("Content-Type")
    unless type && SSE.event_stream?(type)
      return SSEError.new("the answer is #{type ? type.inspect : "untyped"}, not text/event-stream", status)
    end
    reader = response.body_io
    while true
      got = reader.read(65536)
      return got unless got.is_a?(String)
      if got.bytesize == 0
        parser.finish
        return false
      end
      parser.feed(got) do |event|
        return true unless block.call(event)
      end
    end
  end

  # Whether a `Content-Type` is `text/event-stream`, in any case, with any
  # parameters.
  def self.event_stream?(type : String) : Bool
    parsed = MIME.parse?(type)
    return false unless parsed
    parsed[0] == "text/event-stream"
  end
end
